# Research a quiet compact multi-tool mechanism

## Scope and existing evidence

Evaluate pen carousels, independent tool heads, registration, vibration, noise,
desk footprint, serviceability, and safe color-change recovery.

## Finite three-mechanism comparison

**Constraints:** optional desk-scale multi-tool writer, one sheet, up to
four pens/markers, explicit safe tool separation, low perceived noise,
serviceable inks and no implicit tool-change motions. The analysis uses
relative qualitative expectations only; none are measured noise or costs.

| Design alternative | Noise hypothesis | Desk footprint | Cost and service | Registration and safety risk |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Four-position rotating pen carousel | Motor/gear indexing noise; potentially brief | Small dock, more height | Moderate hardware, complex alignment | Angular indexing error, pinch zone and adjacent-tip collisions |
| Four-slot linear side rack with one XY carrier | Side-to-side docking noise; predictable stops | More width, low height | Simple replaceable slots; moderate precision | Wrong-tool pickup, rack strikes and uncapped tip leakage |
| Four independent tool heads on one rail | Multiple drives and vibration sources | Wide and mechanically heavy | Highest wiring/control and maintenance effort | Carriage collisions, duplicate active pens and interlock complexity |

**Decision:** use the **linear side rack** only as the preferred conceptual
prototype because each individual slot can be inspected and replaced and the
single carrier retains one active pen. Reject it if a realistic desk-width
envelope cannot be met; a carousel then becomes a separate engineering
alternative, not an assumed interchange. Neither is known quieter or safer
without acoustic and mechanical evidence. The independent-head design is
outside a first prototype because it multiplies motion and collision states.

**Bounded work example:** design one four-tool rack at a reserved park edge.
The carrier moves pen-up to a known park point, confirms `ToolReleased`,
requests slot 2, checks pickup identity, and leaves the dock only after
position/contact interlocks admit it. A tool misidentification, protruding
marker cap, uncertain carriage XY, saturated collision envelope or missed
release sensor refuses the swap without resuming any pen-down operation.

**Future measurement gates:** one desk-outline check against a declared
operator limit, audible-event and vibration traces per swap, four-slot
repeatability and tool-offset drift, deliberate wrong-tool pickup, service
cycle for dried ink, leak/cleaning containment, and emergency-stop refusal.
Declare sample count and measurement equipment before tests. A hand-waving
"quiet" rating never passes acoustic acceptance.

**Assumptions and limits:** this is architecture comparison without CAD,
component pricing, noise metering, kinematics, prototype, supplier evidence,
physical device, or electrical safety assessment. No future multi-tool
mechanism is permitted to redefine the accepted single-pen v1 contract.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical three-design comparison, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** relative size/noise/cost/service/safety matrix, a chosen
  prototype concept, explicit failed-pickup refusal and measurement gates.
- **Unavailable:** measured acoustic level, real cost, physical dimensions,
  docking reliability and any manufactured device.
